The moment runs. Stopping here as instructed.

## What the moment is

**The bolt passes the window, then the window divides into the shop.**

A single tall aperture is cut into the page and held there while you scroll. Behind it, one continuous bolt of cloth runs sideways — oxford, jersey, canvas, brushed wool, twill, indigo denim, lambswool, alpaca, washed cotton — with a running caption naming the weave currently in the window (`01/09 — PLAIN BASKET, 2/2 — 140gsm oxford, Portugal`). At 62% through the scene the one window divides into nine. While they open, the bolt **stays fixed in the page**, so the nine panes are holes onto one continuous cloth, not nine copies of a crop. Only once they're apart does each pane travel along the bolt to its own cloth and settle as a product card with name, category and price. The pin releases onto that board — a 3×3 wall of nine cloths on desktop, nine full-width bands on mobile. Entry, division, exit, all off one scroll position.

## What material it moves

`public/cloth/bolt.webp` — an 8100×1300 bolt (1.8 MB; a 4050×650 variant is served under 760px). Nine cloth plates I authored and rendered before writing any of the scene: real weave structures (2/2 basket for the oxford, 3/1 warp-faced twill with slub for the 13.5oz selvedge denim, stockinette loops for the knits, brushed fibre over the wool), each in that garment's stated colourway, lit as hanging cloth with irregular vertical folds, then graded as one family in ffmpeg and stitched into a single bitmap. Generator kept at `tools/weave.html`. Type (Instrument Serif, Inter) is self-hosted in `public/type`.

## What drives it

One scroll position → one `rAF`-throttled writer. Per pane it sets a frame transform (FLIP from the real CSS-grid cell to the single window), an inverse scale on an inner wrapper so the cloth never stretches, and a `translate3d` on the shared bolt image. One owner per animated property; no library. Scrubbing through the split held 16.7 ms frames (median, p90 and max) in headless Chrome. Reduced motion is a different build of the same idea, not a disabled one: no pin, no scrub, the nine cards laid out immediately, each already framed on its own cloth — tested separately with the media emulated, and the board is identical to where the motion lands.

## What I could not get

**Photographic garments.** This was the real constraint and it changed the art direction. Pexels and Unsplash search pages and APIs are blocked at this sandbox's proxy (403/Cloudflare; the `napi` endpoint 307s to "Authorization required"), only their bare image CDNs are reachable and I have no photo IDs or key. Wikimedia Commons and Openverse are reachable and I searched both across all nine garments — contact sheets are in `.work/` — but they yield museum accession scans, factory floors, Uniqlo shop racks and the town of Cardigan, Wales. No coherent nine-garment set exists there, and no image generator is callable in this host. So the shop presents each garment by its **cloth**, authored to its own fabric statement and captioned as such on screen: *"Cloth plates drawn to each fabric statement — representations, not photographs of stock."* Nothing claims to be a photograph of this fictional inventory.

Consequently the cards carry cloth, name, category and price — **not** a garment silhouette. If the rest of the page gets built, the size grid, sold-out states, filter and bag all attach to these cards; whether cloth-only product tiles are acceptable in place of garment views is the call to make before that.

Two treatments were compared: a cinematic lookbook (video/clip-scrub cross-fading garments), rejected on material grounds — no footage is obtainable here and generated video is not available, so it would have been a placeholder; and this one, the aperture/bolt, which is buildable at full fidelity from material I can author and whose transition *is* the shopping view rather than a fade into one.

